# NOBORU DEPLOYMENT RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: HIGH

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs deployment configuration, environment management, build scripts, and production runtime settings for Noboru.

Every change to scripts/, vercel.json, next.config.ts, or deployment-related infrastructure must follow this document and docs/deployment.md.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* Predictable, repeatable deployments to Vercel
* Safe environment variable management across local, preview, and production
* Build and runtime configuration remain explicit and reviewable
* Scripts automate operations without hiding domain logic
* Production releases protect user learning data and auth boundaries
* Noboru can scale deployment complexity without chaos

Deploy systems.

Not manual rituals.

⸻

REQUIREMENTS

Hosting

* Vercel is the primary hosting platform for the Next.js application
* Preview deployments must use isolated environment configuration
* Production deployments require reviewed configuration and passing CI

Environment Management

* Required variables documented in docs/deployment.md
* Secrets stored in Vercel project settings or approved secret stores
* Never commit .env.local, credentials, or private keys
* Separate public and server-only variables clearly

Next Configuration

* next.config.ts changes must be intentional and documented when non-default
* Image domains, redirects, headers, and experimental flags require review
* Do not disable security headers for convenience

Vercel Configuration

* vercel.json defines routing, headers, redirects, and build behavior as needed
* Keep configuration minimal and aligned with Next.js defaults where possible

Scripts

* scripts/ contains operational automation: seed helpers, migrations wrappers, asset checks, release utilities
* Scripts must not embed business rules that belong in services
* Scripts must fail clearly and avoid partial destructive operations without safeguards

Database and Supabase

* Schema changes deploy through supabase/migrations/, not ad hoc production edits
* Production data operations require explicit procedure in docs/deployment.md

Build Safety

* Production builds must succeed with strict TypeScript
* Failing tests in protected domains block release per docs/testing-strategy.md

Observability

* Deployment docs define logging and failure monitoring expectations
* Do not log secrets during deploy or runtime bootstrap

⸻

CODING STANDARDS

Scripts

* Use explicit Node or shell entry points with usage comments
* Accept environment via process.env, not hardcoded paths or credentials
* Exit non-zero on failure
* Idempotent where repeated execution is possible

Configuration Files

* Comment non-obvious settings briefly
* Group related settings together
* Avoid experimental features without documented reason

Secrets

* Load at runtime on server only
* Validate required env vars during startup for server processes where practical

Preview vs Production

* Preview may use staging Supabase or isolated datasets as defined in docs/deployment.md
* Never point preview builds at production databases without explicit approved process

Performance

* Respect architecture targets during deploy config: initial load, route change, interaction latency

⸻

SUCCESS CRITERIA

Deployment work is successful when:

* Local, preview, and production environments are clearly documented
* Builds deploy to Vercel without manual undocumented steps
* Secrets remain outside the repository
* next.config.ts and vercel.json changes are reviewable and minimal
* Scripts are safe, named clearly, and reusable
* Database migrations align with deployed application versions
* docs/deployment.md reflects the live process

⸻

FAILURE CRITERIA

Deployment quality is degraded when any of the following occur:

* Secrets committed or printed during deploy scripts
* Manual production schema edits outside migrations
* Undocumented required environment variables
* Scripts that mutate production data without confirmation safeguards
* Preview environments accidentally using production credentials
* Build configuration disables type or lint enforcement without approval
* Fragile one-off deploy steps known only to one developer
* next.config.ts changes that break offline or auth behavior without review

When failure criteria appear, fix deployment discipline before shipping features.

⸻

EXAMPLES

Correct script

scripts/check-env.ts validates required variables and exits with readable errors.

Correct secret handling

SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY configured only in Vercel production environment settings.

Correct migration workflow

Apply supabase migration locally, commit migration file, deploy app version expecting new schema.

Incorrect script

Script deletes user progress tables without backup or environment guard.

Incorrect config

Disabling TypeScript build errors in next.config.ts to unblock release.

Incorrect env practice

Committing .env.production with live credentials.

⸻

ANTI-PATTERNS

Secrets in git

Deploy scripts with embedded business logic

Silent failure in operational scripts

Shared production and preview credentials

Manual hotfixes without migration files

Unreviewed experimental Next flags in production

Running destructive scripts against prod by default

Undocumented one-step "just run this" deployment notes

Disabling CI checks to force deploy

Configuration sprawl across unreviewed local-only changes

